        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe core of the diff is clean and idiomatic: the `membershipLevel` computation stays exactly where `membershipTier` lived \u2014 as a derived MapStruct `@Mapping` expression right beside `membershipNumber`, mirroring that pattern \u2014 and the openapi schema is updated coherently (type, bounds, example, description). No duplication is introduced, and the audit line (`AuditOwnerCreated`) never recorded the tier, so it correctly needs no change. Good work there.\n\nThe problem is what the change orphaned:\n\n- **`AssignOwnerHouseholdSize` no longer has any consumer.** The old `membershipTier` expression was the *only* reader of `householdSize` (`getHouseholdSize() >= 3` for GOLD). The new `membershipLevel` formula uses only `email` and `namesakeCount` \u2014 it never touches `householdSize`. So after this change, the `AssignOwnerHouseholdSize` step (wired into `owners.POST.yml`) and the `household_size` column it populates exist purely to persist a value that nothing in application logic reads or returns.\n\n- **The remaining javadoc lost its reason to exist.** The diff removed the line `A household of three or more members makes the owner {@code GOLD}.` \u2014 which was the *entire justification* for computing `householdSize`. What's left describes *what* the step does but no longer *why* the value matters. A maintainer reading it would reasonably ask \"why do we still compute this?\"\n\nSuggested resolution (pick one, don't leave it dangling):\n- If `household_size` is no longer needed, remove the `AssignOwnerHouseholdSize` step, its wiring in `owners.POST.yml`, and (if truly unused) the `householdSize` field/column \u2014 the cleanest, most coherent outcome now that its consumer is gone.\n- If the persisted column must stay, update the `AssignOwnerHouseholdSize` javadoc to state the surviving reason it's populated, so the step doesn't read as motivationless dead computation."
